>   I don't understand why people object to Stormbringer attacking everything
> in sight. Since only chaotic characters can wield it, no harm is done unless
> it us used on a chaotic priest.

Au contrair.
I am still playing a game in which, as a neutral barbarian, I used Stormbringer
for quite a while.  I got it off a ghost level.  Sure, it blasted me every
time I wielded it, but I had a lot of hps, I didn't unwield it very often,
and I didn't have any other good weapons at the time.
(At one point I was a vampire lord, and drained two levels with each hit...
what fun!)

> >This discussion is irritating, IMHO.  The whole idea of making Stormbringer 
> >was to make it like the Michael Moorcock version, which attacked 
> >everything.  The sword is SUPPOSED to be unreliable.  In the books, 
> >Stormbringer kills friend and foe alike, much to Elric's chagrin.

(So why didn't Elric throw it away?  Or was it welded to his hand? :-)

In the first post on this topic, I actually suggested making
Stormbringer MORE unreliable, e.g. attacking monsters when you don't
move toward them.  What I object to is that Stormbringer's
unreliability currently takes the form of a crippling of the
interface, which can be overcome through monotonous use of ';'.
Stormbringer should either become a REAL factor (this sounds like the
most popular option), or stop being annoying.

I see two ways it can become a real factor:
  1) The wielder can no longer tell whether a creature is tame/peaceful/not;
  2) The wielder sometimes attacks adjacent creatures unintentionally.

The latter seems more in line with Stormbringer's personality.  This
would mean that a player would have to unwield Stormbringer, or even
put it in a bag, when going near shopkeepers, priests, the oracle,
watchmen, peaceful Orcus, sleeping nymphs, their pet, a giant when
player is satiated, or anyone else they didn't want to land a stray
blow on.

The question is, how often would Stormbringer initiate attacks?
And should these attacks take up the player's turn?

I would say, not too often.  And maybe only on turns when the player
is not attacking.  An earlier poster's idea about hunger increasing
with time sounds good.  Stormbringer's hunger would only be satisfied
by "drawing the life" from something; so missing, or hitting an undead
creature, would not.
The hungrier Stormbringer is, the more likely it is, per turn, to take
a swipe at a neighboring creature.
I don't think the attacks should take up the player's turn.

Another question -- does Stormbringer have enough life of its own
to keep attacking when the player is unconscious?
This would add another dimension of both advantage and disadvantage
to wielding Stormy...

